DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The ppiiddffiillee family of functions allows daemons to handle PID files.  It
     uses flopen(3) to lock a pidfile and detect already running daemons.

     The ppiiddffiillee__ooppeenn() function opens (or creates) a file specified by the
     _p_a_t_h argument and locks it.  If _p_i_d_p_t_r argument is not NULL and file can
     not be locked, the function will use it to store a PID of an already run‐
     ning daemon or -1 in case daemon did not write its PID yet.  The function
     does not write process' PID into the file here, so it can be used before
     ffoorrkk()ing and exit with a proper error message when needed.  If the _p_a_t_h
     argument is NULL, _/_v_a_r_/_r_u_n_/⟨_p_r_o_g_n_a_m_e⟩_._p_i_d file will be used.  The
     ppiiddffiillee__ooppeenn() function sets the O_CLOEXEC close-on-exec flag when open‐
     ing the pidfile.

     The ppiiddffiillee__wwrriittee() function writes process' PID into a previously opened
     file.  The file is truncated before write, so calling the ppiiddffiillee__wwrriittee()
     function multiple times is supported.

     The ppiiddffiillee__cclloossee() function closes a pidfile.  It should be used after
     daemon ffoorrkk()s to start a child process.

     The ppiiddffiillee__rreemmoovvee() function closes and removes a pidfile.

     The ppiiddffiillee__ffiilleennoo() function returns the file descriptor for the open
     pidfile.

RREETTUURRNN VVAALLUUEESS
     The ppiiddffiillee__ooppeenn() function returns a valid pointer to a _p_i_d_f_h structure
     on success, or NULL if an error occurs.  If an error occurs, _e_r_r_n_o will
     be set.

     The ppiiddffiillee__wwrriittee(), ppiiddffiillee__cclloossee(), and ppiiddffiillee__rreemmoovvee() functions re‐
     turn the value 0 if successful; otherwise the value -1 is returned and
     the global variable _e_r_r_n_o is set to indicate the error.

     The ppiiddffiillee__ffiilleennoo() function returns the low-level file descriptor.  It
     returns -1 and sets _e_r_r_n_o if a NULL _p_i_d_f_h is specified, or if the pidfile
     is no longer open.

EEXXAAMMPPLLEESS
     The following example shows in which order these functions should be
     used.  Note that it is safe to pass NULL to ppiiddffiillee__wwrriittee(),
     ppiiddffiillee__rreemmoovvee(), ppiiddffiillee__cclloossee() and ppiiddffiillee__ffiilleennoo() functions.

     struct pidfh *pfh;
     pid_t otherpid, childpid;

     pfh = pidfile_open("/var/run/daemon.pid", 0600, &otherpid);
     if (pfh == NULL) {
             if (errno == EEXIST) {
                     errx(EXIT_FAILURE, "Daemon already running, pid: %jd.",
                         (intmax_t)otherpid);
             }
             /* If we cannot create pidfile from other reasons, only warn. */
             warn("Cannot open or create pidfile");
             /*
              * Even though pfh is NULL we can continue, as the other pidfile_*
              * function can handle such situation by doing nothing except setting
              * errno to EDOOFUS.
              */
     }

     if (daemon(0, 0) == -1) {
             warn("Cannot daemonize");
             pidfile_remove(pfh);
             exit(EXIT_FAILURE);
     }

     pidfile_write(pfh);

     for (;;) {
             /* Do work. */
             childpid = fork();
             switch (childpid) {
             case -1:
                     syslog(LOG_ERR, "Cannot fork(): %s.", strerror(errno));
                     break;
             case 0:
                     pidfile_close(pfh);
                     /* Do child work. */
                     break;
             default:
                     syslog(LOG_INFO, "Child %jd started.", (intmax_t)childpid);
                     break;
             }
     }

     pidfile_remove(pfh);
     exit(EXIT_SUCCESS);

EERRRROORRSS
     The ppiiddffiillee__ooppeenn() function will fail if:

     [EEXIST]           Some process already holds the lock on the given pid‐
                        file, meaning that a daemon is already running.  If
                        _p_i_d_p_t_r argument is not NULL the function will use it
                        to store a PID of an already running daemon or -1 in
                        case daemon did not write its PID yet.

     [ENAMETOOLONG]     Specified pidfile's name is too long.

     [EINVAL]           Some process already holds the lock on the given pid‐
                        file, but PID read from there is invalid.

     The ppiiddffiillee__ooppeenn() function may also fail and set _e_r_r_n_o for any errors
     specified for the fstat(2), open(2), and read(2) calls.

     The ppiiddffiillee__wwrriittee() function will fail if:

     [EDOOFUS]          Improper function use.  Probably called before
                        ppiiddffiillee__ooppeenn().

     The ppiiddffiillee__wwrriittee() function may also fail and set _e_r_r_n_o for any errors
     specified for the fstat(2), ftruncate(2), and write(2) calls.

     The ppiiddffiillee__cclloossee() function may fail and set _e_r_r_n_o for any errors speci‐
     fied for the close(2) and fstat(2) calls.

     The ppiiddffiillee__rreemmoovvee() function will fail if:

     [EDOOFUS]          Improper function use.  Probably called not from the
                        process which made ppiiddffiillee__wwrriittee().

     The ppiiddffiillee__rreemmoovvee() function may also fail and set _e_r_r_n_o for any errors
     specified for the close(2), fstat(2), write(2), and unlink(2) system
     calls and the flopen(3) library function.

     The ppiiddffiillee__ffiilleennoo() function will fail if:

     [EDOOFUS]          Improper function use.  Probably called not from the
                        process which used ppiiddffiillee__ooppeenn().

